WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:01.590
Okay.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:06.610
We've been talking about these sort of like
four meta issues in American politics for

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:07.730
a while now.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.080
One of those is climate change, climate change.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.300
If we don't deal with it, uh, it, it impacts
everything else that we care about.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.890
If your city's underwater, it's hard to be
an activist for education policy is the way

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:22.500
I like to, uh, have sort of apply a metaphor
or an example to it.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.150
Net neutrality and Internet freedom is another
Meta issue.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:33.270
We learn about most of what's going on in
the world, um, through the news media and

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:40.600
through the Internet and through a websites
and from news stations that are so impacted

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.559
by net neutrality and internet freedom.

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:47.420
So that's another Meta issue that affects
a lot of a, of what we care about.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.560
Our electoral system is a third, the fact
that we have an electoral college and everything

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.620
that connects to that, including voter suppression,
voter Id, the way, votes are counted the way

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.710
primaries are handled, all of that stuff.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.579
So the electoral system would be like Meta
issue number three.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:06.509
And then you've got campaign finance, which
rounds out the list.

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:11.729
The way politics is financed in the United
States, a big part of campaign finance is

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:12.799
citizens United.

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:15.130
Now, it's not the only part.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:20.259
It's not the only reason why we have the system
of campaign finance that we have today, but

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:21.700
it is a big part of it.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.289
And Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff has
now introduced a constitutional amendment

00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:31.990
to overturn citizens United Citizens United
is the 2010 supreme court decision that did

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.889
away with corporate campaign spending restrictions.

00:01:35.889 --> 00:01:41.240
And if this constitutional amendment were
ratified, it would allow limits to be put

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.779
back in place by Congress and by states on
the contributions that companies can make.

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:52.520
And it would let states decide if, for example,
they want to make public financing of elections,

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.820
the law of the land.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.159
So this is huge.

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:57.569
It's great news.

00:01:57.569 --> 00:01:58.670
It's the right thing.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.630
We should support it, but there are caveats.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:08.030
Caveat number one is this is going nowhere
for as long as Republicans control the Senate

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.709
and for as long as Republican a Republican
is in the White House, that's number one.

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:15.689
But it's still good to get this going.

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:20.210
And I support Adam Schiff doing it because
we've been talking about a constitutional

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:23.430
amendment for a long time at the same.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.959
It's really important to understand, and this
is a great opportunity to do it, that there

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:33.920
are many other things we can do to deal with
the money in politics, status quo, which is

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.840
a disaster in the United States that are separate
from the repeal of citizens United.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.630
Number one, we can change our voting system
in ways that would be a huge step in the right

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:45.630
direction.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:50.359
Some of the specifics about how that can be
done, we've talked about on the show already.

00:02:50.359 --> 00:02:55.799
The point is that's totally separate from
citizens United and it could lead to significant

00:02:55.799 --> 00:03:02.749
improvements in the sort of election status
quo, uh, of the United States of America.

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:05.090
Number two, we could get rid of the electoral
college.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:12.940
The National Popular Vote Interstate Compact
is about 70% of the way to achieving 270 electoral

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:19.840
votes, which would allow with no change to
the constitution, a de facto national popular

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:20.840
vote.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.400
That's something else we can continue working
on.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.400
Number three, gerrymandering.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.670
Yes, gerrymandering is connected to money
in politics, but gerrymandering is also connected

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.969
to many other things, including the census,
which is upcoming in 2020.

00:03:33.969 --> 00:03:41.530
Um, and in general, uh, doing redistricting
with a nonpartisan or bipartisan commission,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:47.829
sometimes employing particular mathematical
algorithms rather than the whimsy of individual

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:49.430
partisan lawmakers.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:55.189
There's a lot that can be done with gerrymandering
that doesn't require repealing a citizen's

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:58.110
United, dealing with voter suppression tactics.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:04.019
This includes everything from voter id, limiting,
limiting, early voting, reducing the number

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:06.510
of polling places on and on and on.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.019
We've talked about all of those things.

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:14.250
Yes, a lot of that connects to a citizens
United and how politics is financed.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:18.299
But we can also accomplish a lot, uh, independently
on that.

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:23.400
But all of this is really to build on fixing
money in politics.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.990
It's not, instead of fixing money in politics,
so we do need to get rid of citizens United.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:33.610
But it's also really important to understand
that in the nine years since citizens United,

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:40.199
uh, became the law of the land, it has opened
the door to a lot of other incremental changes.

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:45.380
Citizens United in and of itself is not the
change that needs to be undone.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:47.580
It's a big change that needs to be undone.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.180
But it has opened the door to a lot of little
changes, getting rid of restrictions on what's

00:04:52.180 --> 00:05:00.360
called express advocacy, um, allowing electioneering
by more organizations, which in and of itself

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.919
led to the creation of many superpacs and
lots of other things.

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:09.120
So, obviously citizens United allowed a lot
of these other things to take place.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.020
But what we need to not forget is that even
if we were to overturn citizens United by

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.720
whatever means, it's ultimately overturned
if it ever is.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.759
You still need to undo a lot of that other
stuff that came from the citizens United ruling.

00:05:22.759 --> 00:05:24.710
And that's a major uphill battle.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:26.430
It's worth doing.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:32.699
It's not impossible, but understand that even
the citizens United repeal is one step of

00:05:32.699 --> 00:05:39.780
probably hundreds just in that pillar of the
broader total reforms that we're advocating

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.360
for so good for Adam Schiff.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.410
This is the right thing.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:49.009
We should push for it, we should support it,
but let's not overstate its importance.

00:05:49.009 --> 00:05:52.979
And most importantly, let's remember that
there are, you know, like six or seven other

00:05:52.979 --> 00:05:58.330
areas that we can be working on right now
simultaneously while we push for getting the

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:02.879
Senate back and getting the White House back,
which will, uh, uh, at least make the path

00:06:02.879 --> 00:06:05.229
to repealing citizens United easier.

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:10.500
The Supreme Court that Donald Trump has appointed
two justices to will be an impediment.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.800
Even if Democrats do take over the Senate
and the White House, but that'll be a problem.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.610
There'll be a bridge we crossed when we come
to it.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:23.620
There are way more things that we can be working
on simultaneously related to fixing elections

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.560
in American politics.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:26.680
Let me know what you think about this.

